; `__indexTrap`: a call that never returns, so it inhabits every result
; type - and the status-77 trap it lowers to.
;
; WHY THIS PRIMITIVE EXISTS. Traps in Axiom are `internal` LLVM
; functions the runtime block emits, so nothing in `stdlib/` could
; reach one. A container that wants to REFUSE an out-of-range index
; rather than answer a value needs exactly that:
; `docs/generics-design.md` section 4 records the decision that
; `vecGet` should trap, and the reason it could not - `(-> (Vec a) Int
; a)` cannot answer 0, because there is no way to make up an `a`.
;
; WHAT THIS FIXTURE PINS, and the first two terms are the type-level
; claim rather than the runtime one:
;
;   1. It inhabits `Int`. `pick` returns `Int` and one arm is the trap.
;   2. It inhabits `String`. The same trap, a different result type, in
;      one program - which is what "never returns" buys and what a
;      concrete `Int` trap could not give. AX3040's own way out.
;   3. The value arms still answer, so the trap has not swallowed the
;      function: `pick 7` is 7 and `name 1` is "ok".
;
; The trap itself - the message, the backtrace and exit 77 - is pinned
; by `464-index-trap.exit` and `.err` beside this file, because a
; fixture that traps cannot also print its own success.
(import IO)

(:: pick (-> Int Int))

(fn (pick n)
  (if (< n 0)
    __indexTrap
    n
  )
)

(:: name (-> Int String))

(fn (name n)
  (if (< n 0)
    __indexTrap
    "ok"
  )
)

(:: main Int)

;@axiom:effect(io)
(fn (main)
  {
    (println (pick 7))
    (println (name 1))
    (println (pick (- 0 1)))
    0
  }
)
